From 026055118a668f4a46b746879fa6717684b8ab4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Groboclown <groboclown@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:44:12 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] forward: limit each ruled host to the ports its rules
 give

Add `ports = [...]` to `[[forward.host]]`. A host whose rules give ports
is reachable only on those ports, for CONNECT and for absolute-form
http:// (port 80 when none is written). Absent `ports` keeps the old
behavior: any port.

What it prevents:

- Reaching an allowed host on any port. Before, a rule for a host
  covered every port, so `CONNECT allowed-host:8080` was intercepted like
  port 443.
- Stepping around a host's rules by changing port. A ruled host on a
  port its rules don't give is refused with 403 whatever the unmatched
  policy.
- Ambiguous port lists. Ports are checked at CONNECT time, before any
  request is known, so every rule naming one host must give the same
  `ports` (or none); an empty list, 0, or a value outside 1-65535 is a
  config error rather than a rule that silently matches nothing.

Ports are kept per host in `ForwardConfig::host_ports`, outside the
policy `RuleSet`. Server-mode policy carries no ports and is unaffected.
Refusals log `reason = "port_not_allowed"` and count under that outcome.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
